<p>
Nietzsche&rsquo;s main criticism of the church in his day was that it was simply about the world to come, and that a religion that was of no value in this world now was of no value at all — a sentiment articulated in Joe Hill&rsquo;s poem &ldquo;The Preacher and the Slave&rdquo;:
</p>

<blockquote>
<p>
Long-haired preachers come out every night,<br>
Try to tell you what’s wrong and what’s right;<br>
But when asked how ’bout something to eat<br>
They will answer with voices so sweet:<br>
<br>
You will eat, bye and bye,<br>
In that glorious land above the sky;<br>
Work and pray, live on hay,<br>
You’ll get pie in the sky when you die&hellip;.
</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
When Jesus read a Jubilee text from Isaiah in the synagogue at Nazareth, he didn&rsquo;t say that someday this would be fulfilled, but that it was fulfilled &ldquo;today.&rdquo;
</p>

<p>
May it be so, even today.
</p>

